9. Reading Excel Data
The current ETL process reads the Excel worksheet using pandas.
Example:
df = pd.read_excel(
    subfiles,
    sheet_name=sheetname,
    header=1
)
The header=1 parameter indicates that the second row of the Excel worksheet is treated as the column header.
This is required because the source MIS files may contain an additional row above the actual column headers.
________________________________________
10. Handling Inconsistent Excel Structure
The source Excel files are not completely standardized.
Different worksheets may contain:
•	Empty columns
•	Different date formats
•	X values
•	Blank cells
•	Different header positions
•	Different sheet names
•	Additional formatting
•	Unused spreadsheet areas
Therefore, the ETL process performs data cleaning before exporting the final datasets.
________________________________________
11. Removing Sparse Columns
Some Excel worksheets contain columns that are mostly empty.
The following operation removes columns that contain fewer than seven non-null values:
df = df.dropna(axis=1, thresh=7)
Explanation
axis=1
means columns.
thresh=7
means that a column must contain at least seven non-null values to be retained.
This helps remove unnecessary columns caused by inconsistent Excel formatting or partially populated worksheets.
________________________________________
12. Handling X Values
Some source worksheets use X or x to represent unavailable or non-applicable values.
These values can be converted to missing values:
df = df.replace(["X", "x"], pd.NA)
After conversion, completely empty columns can be removed:
df = df.dropna(axis=1, how="all")
This means:
If every value in a column is missing after replacing X values, the entire column is removed.
For example:
Column A
--------
X
X
X
X
becomes:
Column A
--------
NA
NA
NA
NA
and can therefore be removed.
A column containing:
X
X
250
X
X
is retained because it contains an actual value.
