13. Kitchen Identification
Because each kitchen has its own folder, the kitchen name is obtained from the parent directory of the Excel file.
Example:
df["Kitchen"] = subfiles.parent.stem
If the file is located at:
ATC/COM.xlsx
the value added to the DataFrame will be:
Kitchen = ATC
This allows data from multiple kitchens to be combined while retaining the source kitchen information.
________________________________________
14. Date Standardization
Date values are one of the important areas of data cleaning because different source files may contain different date formats.
Examples include:
2025-07-01
2025-07-01 00:00:00
01-Jun-2026
01/06/2026
01-06-2026
If these values are exported without standardization, Power BI may interpret some of them differently or fail to parse them.
Therefore, dates are standardized before exporting the data.
________________________________________
15. Date Cleaning Function
A reusable function is used to identify supported date formats.
def clean_date(value):

    if pd.isna(value):
        return pd.NaT

    value = str(value).strip()

    formats = [
        "%Y-%m-%d",
        "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S",
        "%d-%b-%Y",
        "%d/%m/%Y",
        "%d-%m-%Y"
    ]

    for fmt in formats:
        try:
            return pd.to_datetime(value, format=fmt)
        except (ValueError, TypeError):
            continue

    return pd.NaT
The function works as follows:
1.	Check whether the value is missing.
2.	Convert the value to text.
3.	Remove unnecessary spaces.
4.	Try the known date formats one by one.
5.	Return the converted date if a format matches.
6.	Return NaT if none of the supported formats match.
________________________________________
16. Applying Date Cleaning
The function can be applied to the entire Date column:
df["Date"] = df["Date"].apply(clean_date)
After conversion, the dates can be exported in a consistent format:
df["Date"] = df["Date"].dt.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
The resulting format is:
YYYY-MM-DD
For example:
01-Jun-2026
becomes:
2026-06-01
________________________________________
17. Handling Missing Dates
Some Excel reports may contain a date only on the first row of a group while subsequent rows are blank.
Forward filling can be used when the source structure indicates that blank rows belong to the preceding date:
df["Date"] = df["Date"].ffill()
This carries the previous valid date into the following blank cells.
The use of ffill() should be based on the actual structure of the source worksheet.
________________________________________
18. Date Validation
After cleaning the Date column, invalid or unrecognized values can be checked.
invalid_dates = df["Date"].isna().sum()
This helps identify records where the date could not be converted.
Invalid records can also be inspected:
bad_dates = df[df["Date"].isna()]
This validation is useful before exporting the data to Power BI.
